{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall surely destroy all the places, etc.</b> This is explained according to what they said in tractate Avodah Zarah, folio 47, and this is the language of the Mishnah: Three categories of houses there are — a house built originally for idolatry is forbidden; if he plastered and decorated it for idolatry and added new construction, one takes what he added, etc. And they said in the Gemara, and this is their language: Rav said — one who bowed to a house forbade it. The inference being that he holds detached and ultimately attached is forbidden. But we teach 'he built it' — he built it even though he did not bow to it. End quote. And so Rambam ruled in chapter 8 of the Laws of Avodah Zarah (halakhah 5), and it is the meaning of the verse here: <b>et all the places where they served there</b> — meaning, every place where they had idolatrous worship there, whether they built it for idolatry or whether they bowed to it and it is built — even though it is attached, it is forbidden and must be destroyed." "And its saying <b>which you are inheriting them</b> is explained according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 7 of the Laws of Avodah Zarah (halakhah 1), and this is his language: And in the land of Israel it is a mitzvah to pursue after it until we destroy it, but outside the land, etc. — every place that we conquer, we must destroy all the idolatry that is in it. End quote. See then that the parameter of the obligation to destroy idolatry is any place that they inherited where it is located, and that is His saying <b>which you are inheriting</b> — for by this parameter all lands are equal." "And the reason for the doubling of ״אַבֵּד תְּאַבְּדוּן״ is explained according to their statement in tractate Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 43b) that nullification of idolatry requires two things: grinding, and scattering to the wind or casting into the sea, and with only one of them one does not discharge his obligation. Therefore He doubled to say two destructions." "And His saying <b>et their gods on the mountains</b> — since He forbade all the places, and we explained that even a built house that is attached is forbidden, He comes to say that there is something attached that is not forbidden even though they worshipped it. And what is it? The mountains. As they expounded in Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 45a), and this is their language: 'Their gods on the mountains' — and not 'the mountains their gods.' And in the chapter of Rabbi Yishmael (Avodah Zarah 51b) they expounded 'all the places' — if it has no application to idolatry, apply it to utensils. End quote. They, may their memory be for blessing, hold that detached and ultimately attached does not require a verse, and there are seventy faces to the Torah." "<b>Further</b>, His saying <b>et their gods</b> may be explained by raising the question of why He interposed the phrase ״which you are inheriting them״ between the mention of the worshippers and the worshipped — for He should have said it this way: ״the nations served there et their gods, etc., which you are inheriting them.״ Indeed the intention is according to what our Sages, may their memory be for blessing, said in tractate Avodah Zarah, folio 44, and this is their language: Rav Huna raised the difficulty — it is written (I Chronicles 14:12) ״וַיִּשָּׂרְפוּ בָאֵשׁ״ and it is written (II Samuel 5:21) ״וַיִּשָּׂאֵם״. There is no contradiction — here, before their god came to me; here, after their god came to me, as it is written ״וַיִּקַּח אֶת עֲטֶרֶת מַלְכָּם מֵעַל רֹאשׁוֹ.״ And who permitted it? Rav Naḥman said: Their god came to me and nullified it. End quote. See then that there exists a possibility to inherit even their gods themselves — that is, when their worshippers were with them and nullified them, Israel may benefit from them. And that is His saying <b>which you are inheriting them</b> — and not them alone but also their gods, and that is His saying <b>et their gods.</b> And the condition of this ruling the verse hinted at by placing the phrase ״them״ first and afterward ״et their gods,״ according to the words of the Talmud, which stated that after their god came to me, David benefited from the crown of their king." "<b>And by way of</b> remez, the verse further intends to allude to the destruction of the hidden power, also through the power of the people of Israel, for when they strengthen themselves with the strengthening of good deeds, they destroy its revealed and hidden aspects from them. And this is what it says: <b>all the places where they served there</b> — meaning, where their worship reaches, for it is the throne of ס״מ the wicked one, to uproot it; for with the fall of this power, the supernal power is strengthened, and HaShem will be King over all the earth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {5 "ס״מ is an abbreviation for סַם מָוֶת or סַטְן מָוֶת, the angel of death / Satan; left as acronym per the translator's convention of preserving the Or HaChaim's exact forms."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:03.823861228Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}